    <h1>AHK-Cu copper peptide: profile and verification</h1>
    <p class="lead muted">AHK-Cu is often treated as a drop-in alternative to GHK-Cu. It is a different tripeptide with its own sequence, copper binding and analytical behaviour — and that difference should show up in the specification.</p>
    <p class="small muted" style="margin-bottom:18px;">Part of the <a href="/knowledge/cosmetic-peptide-raw-materials-framework/">cosmetic peptide raw materials pillar</a>.</p>

    <h2>What it is</h2>
    <p>AHK-Cu is a copper complex of the tripeptide alanyl-histidyl-lysine. Like GHK-Cu, it is blue because of the copper–peptide complex; the sequence difference changes the complex's behaviour, including colour, solubility and the analytical signals used to confirm identity.</p>

    <h2>AHK-Cu vs GHK-Cu: what actually differs</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>GHK-Cu</th><th>AHK-Cu</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Peptide sequence</td><td>Gly-His-Lys</td><td>Ala-His-Lys</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Analytical identity</td><td>Mass consistent with GHK + Cu</td><td>Mass consistent with AHK + Cu</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Colour</td><td>Blue complex</td><td>Blue complex</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Formulation behaviour</td><td>pH-sensitive; chelator interactions</td><td>Same class of issues; confirm in your vehicle</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>The shared class behaviour does not make them interchangeable. If a supplier substitutes one for the other without telling you, the COA identity test is the place to catch it.</p>

    <h2>Verification points</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Identity:</strong> MS must match the AHK sequence with copper, not GHK.</li>
      <li><strong>Copper content:</strong> confirms the complex, not just the peptide.</li>
      <li><strong>Purity:</strong> HPLC chromatogram with method; copper complexes can show broad or shifted peaks.</li>
      <li><strong>Counter-ion:</strong> stated and consistent with the mass interpretation.</li>
      <li><strong>Appearance:</strong> colour within an agreed range against a reference.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Formulation questions to ask</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>What is the recommended pH window for this complex?</li>
      <li>How does it behave with chelators and reducing agents in your vehicle?</li>
      <li>What colour range should be expected at the intended concentration?</li>
      <li>What is the microbial status of the lot?</li>
    </ol>


    <h2>Copper content: what to ask</h2>
    <p>Peptide purity does not confirm the complex. Ask for copper content by a stated method (atomic absorption or ICP), and check that it is consistent with the declared complex. A batch with the right peptide mass but the wrong copper load is not the material you specified.</p>

    <h2>Substitution risk</h2>
    <p>Because GHK-Cu and AHK-Cu are both blue copper tripeptides, substitution is easy to miss on appearance alone. The identity test is the control: the MS must match the AHK sequence, not GHK. If a supplier cannot show the spectrum, the price advantage is not worth the ambiguity.</p>

    <h2>Editor's take</h2>
    <p>AHK-Cu and GHK-Cu look interchangeable in a photograph and are not interchangeable in a specification. The identity spectrum is the only reliable divider.</p>

    <h2>Limitations</h2>
    <p>This is formulation research guidance; no cosmetic efficacy claim is made. Finished-product safety and claims are the formulator responsibility.</p>


    <h2>Handling and storage</h2>
    <p>Treat AHK-Cu like other copper peptide complexes: sealed, dry, dark and cool. The powder is hygroscopic, so weigh quickly in a low-humidity environment and reseal immediately. Avoid contact with bare metal; use glass or low-binding plastic. Record the lot, mass, solvent and pH for every preparation so a colour or solubility change can be traced to a cause rather than a suspicion.</p>
    <p>If a batch arrives with a colour outside the agreed range, do not formulate first and investigate later. Photograph the vial against the reference, request the copper content and purity data, and hold the lot until the discrepancy is explained.</p>

